#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133602 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16e43d27-ffaf-58ea-bb6e-664b70770003" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Span of Two Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "c430a11a-8595-568f-9768-aad8a8de415f" :question/math-academy-id "e-5581" :question/problem "Consider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $\\mathcal B$ of $V,$ given below. Given that $\\mathcal B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\dfrac{a}{c}?$\n\n![](images/23/23aedd2796ca55056409a6d0c32e0d5521283297398bd78d8c7ac3ce84c42fba.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n![](images/10/10e08ad8dd61b256018aa33488f94b563669fa91c739785488306d15160838c9.svg)\n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n- **Step 1**: We set ![](images/6e/6ec9d05c93c9ae7a3c3079d69b7c0b5d665fb295efaa918cea26f77988110342.svg)\n\n- **Step 2**: Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_2$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf v_2$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 1+5\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ \\frac{13}{3} \\\\ \\frac{10}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/a9/a9fa0f03fc16c2d3f1c2102033c0ec6c7d4911ee2e1001a460893c85171204b8.svg) is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\dfrac 7 3,$ $c=\\dfrac{10}{3}$ and  \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{c} = \\dfrac{\\left(-\\dfrac{7}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\dfrac{10}{3}\\right)} = -\\dfrac{7}{10}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133603 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b15719b6-0ce4-5fb8-80be-abcaf9344a10" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Column Space of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "47fadc87-f265-5f1c-827a-28e5be9e8121" :question/math-academy-id "e-5582" :question/problem "Consider the subspace $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $\\mathcal B$ of $\\textrm{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $\\mathcal B$ are given below. Given that $\\mathcal B$ is derived from $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\dfrac{c}{a}?$\n\n![](images/b2/b20aeaf492050b26176c993d64442f214c39d24092989ec1c75b5e2ca9673947.svg)" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n![](images/fa/fa7bd1a6125c6796bd5689409a22c34081e78027dbaff1356efecddfe755b323.svg)\n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n- **Step 1**: We set ![](images/0f/0f65ef9e505d3e961d0330c1ff6d52d5239eff91811a61b4878d6a2f13923bff.svg)\n\n- **Step 2**: Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_2$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf v_2$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-5)\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot (-2)+(-3)\\cdot 2}{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ![](images/be/be2842c481ecd55360f9bc3054e2535d4a92bbb8ebd77f169522c329ff2d715d.svg) is an orthogonal basis for $\\textrm{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=-4,$ $c=-1,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{c}{a} = \\dfrac{-1}{-4} = \\dfrac{1}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133604 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ccdefb8e-1bb5-52e9-812d-d2a5bffff731" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthonormal Basis of a Vector Space"}] :question/id #uuid "60dbc829-01fc-5283-8741-68af483d5313" :question/math-academy-id "e-5583" :question/problem "Consider the subspace $V$ and an **orthonormal** basis $\\mathcal B$ of $V,$ given below. Given that $\\mathcal B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$\n'\n![](images/3a/3ae8f3b146aea78019b441917a80b2da118044c207cbf42c4b9fe2b51c3920d2.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n![](images/f0/f04136fae84ebaf8ac272b48ae3d6fda95c26df1a4c3c1c800d261ab8acf0821.svg)\n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n- **Step 1**: We set ![](images/7e/7e7c38a548b58edae501a2f881bb9feb0553eb9ae51a9de96726233a9ce0543b.svg)\n\n- **Step 2**: Next, we find $\\mathbf{v}_2$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for $\\mathbf v_2$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{6\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- ![](images/c2/c2e0f7b0e96c4d945bbf80d262b68366db85df52a754410d39602f4ba37fc4d4.svg)\n\n- ![](images/8c/8c16a541393a8060313140e677fc1e9347b7145c505f7ddb5bdae2111f68e216.svg)\n\nTherefore, ![](images/06/06d515f38454768bd2ec372b917828bbb7a99ee07ea5aa4745a10e5ff1ecdfcf.svg) is an orthonormal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=\\dfrac{2}{3},$ $b=-\\dfrac1  3,$ $c=\\dfrac{2}{3},$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c| = \\left| \\dfrac{2}{3} \\right| + \\left|-\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right| + \\left| \\dfrac{2}{3} \\right| = \\dfrac 5 3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481350 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16e43d27-ffaf-58ea-bb6e-664b70770003" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Span of Two Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35fb792f-f499-55f0-beb5-0a36ea500f74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480845} {:answer/id #uuid "391ed7ae-6243-537b-a07f-f3dade005876" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480852} {:answer/id #uuid "8fdbf6e7-3003-543d-b80a-6884375af46b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481009} {:answer/id #uuid "91a07927-047f-58d6-9e1b-00da5c4d3b96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481011} {:answer/id #uuid "b06c78e9-7124-5c1a-ba75-ab5286b77cd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b06c78e9-7124-5c1a-ba75-ab5286b77cd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481072} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5e8feed-0144-5565-9376-5bcd8f09bbb0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481303}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "17e0153f-17ba-57ee-a920-3117ebdc5b12" :question/math-academy-id "q-140524" :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-1)}{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=1,$ $b=-2,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{-2}{1}=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272123 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b15719b6-0ce4-5fb8-80be-abcaf9344a10" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Column Space of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c5324c4-d372-51db-b80e-57310ecef452" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269376} {:answer/id #uuid "2b563aea-36bf-58e1-85e1-370632786989" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269659} {:answer/id #uuid "339f0949-f255-5abb-92bb-95bd0cafba47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269740} {:answer/id #uuid "4e62fc44-ef13-513b-9718-e526f4879824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269977} {:answer/id #uuid "b9c5a456-558c-5e5d-b7c5-b3d293046719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270909}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e62fc44-ef13-513b-9718-e526f4879824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269977} :answer-field/id #uuid "8085e6de-aa0c-5e0f-84f3-9fd3a3ba5f78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "395132aa-e570-57bb-9869-07e6441f4e7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-140526" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 2}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=2,$ $c=-1,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{c}=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272130 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ccdefb8e-1bb5-52e9-812d-d2a5bffff731" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthonormal Basis of a Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f0be9bb-0dd7-5727-a685-7067529f3b9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269405} {:answer/id #uuid "0fc8fc9a-3735-5af4-ab4b-7a0b06f36be6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269410} {:answer/id #uuid "14837d79-0685-5b2f-b7ae-de1bb6acd6e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269449} {:answer/id #uuid "22a41e2d-2e61-5f3e-9b7c-55de1bb113c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269569} {:answer/id #uuid "a51a7384-da8d-59ed-9c61-4348968fe7f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fc8fc9a-3735-5af4-ab4b-7a0b06f36be6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269410} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa412d3d-18c8-523e-8f62-f8bca8e7c4b4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2e6d1632-87e6-54dd-a8f9-23d096c3bcc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-140539" :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$  \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$  \n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{0\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot 1}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis:\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{1}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{6}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} \\\\ -\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an  orthonormal basis of $V.$   \n\nFinally, $a=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},$ $b=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},$ $c=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}|+|\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}|+|-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}|=\\sqrt{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186414751 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16e43d27-ffaf-58ea-bb6e-664b70770003" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Span of Two Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18fa685c-26d9-50c3-9545-5630e0f95fcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414724} {:answer/id #uuid "194427ad-263d-5d8c-83c2-9e6a530a1d0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414725} {:answer/id #uuid "5c1253b4-be89-5b75-90d7-9bf16f52d17f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414729} {:answer/id #uuid "a926e36b-6239-5512-942a-761db7b00ee5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414740} {:answer/id #uuid "f308a53e-6685-5d66-b2f6-d1b95a0af889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c1253b4-be89-5b75-90d7-9bf16f52d17f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414729} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ce877fc-f404-5538-a461-7601462b178c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186414748}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "44c51145-6075-534b-b54c-46371542f3eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-40737" :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote \n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 3+2\\cdot 0+(-3)\\cdot (-1)}{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{3}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 2 \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, we have $a=-\\frac{1}{2},$ $b=2,$ and \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{2}{(-\\frac{1}{2})}=-4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b15719b6-0ce4-5fb8-80be-abcaf9344a10" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Column Space of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10cc83e4-4a68-52d4-b338-032fbdc0c4f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324125} {:answer/id #uuid "3f470bd7-a807-5528-acc5-2a476fd2064d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324480} {:answer/id #uuid "712b96f9-5e05-5d1a-bb65-c463f83dfa57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324904} {:answer/id #uuid "af04c33b-d58b-555f-af8d-a4f9508a0b5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325459} {:answer/id #uuid "bf00d910-6f67-537f-8bac-b8822a589211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf00d910-6f67-537f-8bac-b8822a589211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325614} :answer-field/id #uuid "180276bb-da02-5515-9e1f-76b1748b74f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d8c7811a-eb0e-522e-b483-fedac22b4a18" :question/math-academy-id "q-40742" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{b}{c}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$ \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{(-3)\\cdot 2+4\\cdot (-1)+(-1)\\cdot 4}{{2}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{4}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]+\\frac{2}{3}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{10}{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ \\frac{10}{3} \\\\ \\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $b=\\frac{10}{3},$ $c=\\frac{5}{3},$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{b}{c}=\\frac{(\\frac{10}{3})}{(\\frac{5}{3})}=2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133795 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ccdefb8e-1bb5-52e9-812d-d2a5bffff731" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthonormal Basis of a Vector Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a5e4e6b-279d-5b72-9f4d-3fba0018faa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366120} {:answer/id #uuid "6f7c8ab6-a501-5b46-8850-8d6264681e04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366121} {:answer/id #uuid "a0e053a2-07c5-5245-8eae-a77ccb16799a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366122} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ad4476-90b2-56e8-ae3c-6cfbde2e981e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366123} {:answer/id #uuid "db5506c3-46ff-5e8a-a19c-64367520311a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366124}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db5506c3-46ff-5e8a-a19c-64367520311a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366124} :answer-field/id #uuid "88d62aee-8dd5-5aa9-8028-53575d48c630" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366119}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dbf56628-94da-5878-ab0e-12187baa0d83" :question/math-academy-id "q-40745" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=\\{{u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an **orthonormal** basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $|a|+|b|+|c|?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{3\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 2}{{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize each vector to get an orthonormal basis.\n\n- ${u}_{1}=\\frac{{v}_{1}}{‖{v}_{1}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- ${u}_{2}=\\frac{{v}_{2}}{‖{v}_{2}‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}}}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}].$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{5} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{5}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthonormal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=-\\frac{1}{3},$ $b=\\frac{2}{3},$ $c=\\frac{2}{3}$ and  \n\n$$\n|a|+|b|+|c|=|-\\frac{1}{3}|+|\\frac{2}{3}|+|\\frac{2}{3}|=\\frac{5}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186481360 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16e43d27-ffaf-58ea-bb6e-664b70770003" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Span of Two Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d9e8e1d-513e-59b9-9746-2585e1481503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480764} {:answer/id #uuid "2466579e-51e6-5724-b11f-494b5cbd91ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480807} {:answer/id #uuid "5e2dc305-ac17-582b-9568-ed63665348b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480914} {:answer/id #uuid "67ccaa28-3669-5421-86da-e8251e588b2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480931} {:answer/id #uuid "d058a80e-4564-5675-b58b-217af9b2adce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e2dc305-ac17-582b-9568-ed63665348b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480914} :answer-field/id #uuid "363499bf-09e7-5ef8-9f85-08b9df560e62" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186481258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41cad912-e122-5581-bcb3-a15aeb801b3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-41031" :question/problem "$$\nV=\\text{Span}\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $V$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $V,$ given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $V$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{c}{a}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 1+0\\cdot (-2)+2\\cdot 1}{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{1}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $V.$\n\nFinally, $a=3,$ $c=1,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{c}{a}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366125 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b15719b6-0ce4-5fb8-80be-abcaf9344a10" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Column Space of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "68d3b6c3-78f1-5dc1-bd02-5e789b94126c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366127} {:answer/id #uuid "85fb58e0-2ba4-5671-87e5-f4ad5d5c3ce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366128} {:answer/id #uuid "4fae99ca-8bd2-52bd-a7a7-6fba7c6f223f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366129} {:answer/id #uuid "b2a6b39d-7fbd-541d-9f3f-312f2e40d9ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366130} {:answer/id #uuid "92e46969-6a43-5755-bf11-d28076f4b84b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2a6b39d-7fbd-541d-9f3f-312f2e40d9ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366130} :answer-field/id #uuid "c32112d1-55af-51b9-b26a-46a43c734125" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186366126}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "46c005f9-5702-59db-bd5c-741e079e67e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-41036" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 4 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}],\\,B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b \\\\ c\\end{aligned}]\\}\n$$\n\nConsider the subspace $\\text{Col}(A)$ and an orthogonal basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(A),$ where $A$ and $B$ are given above. Given that $B$ is derived from $\\text{Col}(A)$ using the Gram-Schmidt process, what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\text{Col}(A)$ is the span of the columns of $A.$  \n\nFirst, let's denote\n\n$\\,$ ${a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{a}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nWe proceed to find an orthogonal basis using the Gram-Schmidt process, as follows.\n\n**Step 1**: We set ${v}_{1}={a}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 2**: Next, we find ${v}_{2}$, by using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{{a}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}}{v}_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the formula for ${v}_{2}$ in our case gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{2} & ={a}_{2}-\\frac{4\\cdot 2+4\\cdot 0+4\\cdot 2}{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{2}^{2}}{v}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\}=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}$ is an orthogonal basis for $\\text{Col}(A).$\n\nFinally, $a=0,$ $b=4,$ and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{0}{4}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351261 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16e43d27-ffaf-58ea-bb6e-664b70770003" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Orthogonal Basis For the Span of Two Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1334ae27-fd25-5463-9af2-ba4ec4924cc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348613} {:answer/id #uuid "60c5e6e0-9b1d-5d73-9ad3-64b43fac8e80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 1759218634932